{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1449175-what-s-wrong-with-1e-pledges-should-i-use-them","id":1449175,"title":"What's wrong with 1e Pledges? Should I use them?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Changeling: The Lost"],"posts":[{"date":"2021-05-21T01:40:09-07:00","id":1449175,"title":"What's wrong with 1e Pledges? Should I use them?","content":"I always thought that both 1e and 2e Pledge systems had their pros and cons. Second edition pledges are simpler and quicker to use, hence they're great when you don't want to go too much into details or slow down the game. First edition pledgecraft however allowed for more detailed and accurate pledges.<br /> <br /> My idea was to use 2e pledge system most of the time but to still use the 1e system for those occasions when the Pledge was a central element of a story, when the details mattered, and when the players would want to customize the terms.<br /> <br /> Then I recently stumbled upon this post by Satchel in another thread:<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1448259-playable-fetches?p=1448655#post1448655\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If what you're instead wondering is why pledges changed to mostly grant boons that you actually had access to, it may have something to do with the multiple times people asked why every changeling wasn't filthy rich and living on easy street despite 1e's implication that the Lost tend to use looser more-exploitable language in pledgecrafting with mortals. There's not much point in being a game about being a fugitive trying to rebuild your life while sticking to a consistent locale and working with magic that tries to keep the books balanced if everyone can wordsmith their way to comfort at character creation; not for nothing does Demon use its similar system with an Integrity analogue that lets them cut and run with increasing frequency; not for nothing does Deviant's power structure include an explicit tagging system for &quot;this game element will attract unwanted attention to you.&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div>That made me wondering: what are the drawbacks of 1e Pledgecraft (other than its complexity)? What kind of pitfalls should I be aware of if I wanted to use it in 2e and how could I avoid them?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26440-moonwhisper","name":"moonwhisper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26440_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-21T09:13:50-07:00","id":1449193,"title":"","content":"Drawbacks of 1e Pledgecraft: it's easy to abuse, the benefits of the pledge come from the Wyrd rather than actually coming from either of the parties involved, it has to be &quot;balanced&quot;.<ul><li>Easy to abuse: it's not difficult to put together pledges that can grant significant bonuses that are effectively permanent with drawbacks that will never come up in play.</li> <li>Benefits of the pledge come from the Wyrd: you can build pledges where the benefits are completely disconnected from the tasks.</li> <li>It has to be &quot;balanced&quot;: this is problematic for two reasons: it's athematic (faeries aren't noted for playing fair) and it removes agency from the players by not allowing them to decide what they'll accept (not everyone views the value of things the same).</li> </ul><br /> <br /> <br /> If forging the Pledge is a major focus I would instead use the Social Maneuvering rules. Each party makes an initial offer of terms, this sets the baseline for the Pledge and can modify Doors (or be a complete non-starter). Offering additional services counts as soft leverage, opening all Doors means the other party accepts the Pledge. The PCs can decide whether they accept the Pledge on their own, without the need for social maneuvering (they can also choose to let the NPCs engage in social maneuvering against them).<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Example</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">The PCs have offended the Summer Queen and are trying to hide from her lackeys in the Hedge; the offense was minor and if they can avoid her for a week she'll calm down and the whole thing will blow over. They flee to Cedric Scourwind's Hollow to hide, offering him a Pledge for their protection. Cedric's side of the deal is to hide the PCs for a week, the PCs offer to take care of Cedric's house and grounds while they're there. Cedric has a Resolve of 3 and Composure 4, so starts with 3 Doors. He hates company (+1 Door) but has a personal grudge against the Summer Queen (-2 Doors), leaving him with 2 Doors. The PCs' impression level with Cedric isn't high enough to allow more than one roll before trouble comes knocking, so they're in a bit of a bind; while an Exceptional Success on their roll would get them in (opening up both doors), it's likely they'll have to offer one (if they get a Success) or two (if they fail) additional favors to Cedric before he'll agree.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> With this system there's tension, room to jockey for an advantageous position, and the ability to either get in over your head or get a lot for very little based on skill and cleverness.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/1452235-proin-s-legacy-hub\" target=\"_blank\">proin's Legacy hub</a>","edited":{"userid":2757,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2757-proindrakenzol","user":"proindrakenzol","date":". 05-21-2021, 12:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2757-proindrakenzol","name":"proindrakenzol","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2757_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1514"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-21T11:37:00-07:00","id":1449201,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>moonwhisper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1449175-what-s-wrong-with-1e-pledges-should-i-use-them#post1449175\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">That made me wondering: what are the drawbacks of 1e Pledgecraft (other than its complexity)?</div> </div> </div> </div>I mentioned Demon's pact system being similar, but the differences there are notable:<br /> <br /> 1. Demons can give mortals vast riches and other benefits that they don't personally possess and have no relation to the agreement besides being things the other party wants with pacts (and in fact that's one of the main bargaining chips it provides), but the only way they can personally benefit from these is to use soul pacts to take over a client's life. Changeling 1e pledgecraft has no such limitation; its main distinction is that conditionals are baked in and you can't just outright offer a one-sided deal like the Unchained can.<br /> <br /> 2. Demons occupy a gameplay setup where exposure is more all-or-nothing and pacts are literally one of an extremely small handful of ways to regain Cover; breaking pledges imperils a changeling's Clarity, and making them offers some extra personal security, but pledges aren't going to help you bounce back from penalty-level Clarity, and being able to be out of your mind and still able to hand out Skill bonuses and Striking Looks as an innate template feature is more appropriate for monsters with a literary niche closer to the True Fae than the fugitive person-made-set-dressing trying to gradually rebuild their life in the mortal world. The Unchained are all <i>about</i> being destabilizing influences who can just disappear into the background and pick up the pieces elsewhere, but the Lost in 1e do poorly by being able to set themselves up in comfort when their relative inability to handle new environments is brought up multiple times.<br /> <br /> <br /> It's telling that the other place an analog to 1e's pledgecrafting system shows up in is the Mage book Summoners, where it's part of a system of bargaining for boons from summoned entities \u2014 and the boons come from the summoned entity, who is generally not a mortal-personlike actor in the story.<br /> <br /> Pledges in Changeling have always been set up such that the consequences of breaking them are more important than mystical benefits derived from them, hence Sealings being a universal fae ability in 2e. The main benefits of swearing an oath to join a motley or a Court or a freehold is that you belong to a group whose members face immediately enforced consequences for betrayal; the bits and bobs you get from strengthening your Mantle are a side benefit to further aligning your behavior to suit your Court's patron or the purpose of the group, and Contract knowledge is a one-time payment for the ability to make a recurring transaction (doubly so for Goblin Contracts).","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><span style=\"font-family:trebuchet ms\"><span style=\"color:#4B0082\"><b>Resident Lore-Hound</b></span><br /> Currently Consuming: <i>Demon: the Descent 1e</i></span>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/111-satchel","name":"Satchel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar111_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"9828"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-21T16:39:33-07:00","id":1449219,"title":"","content":"Because of the way 1e assigned value to different aspects of the pledge, and because those values had to sum to 0 with no further stipulation on how they interacted, you could make pledges that had very strict requirements with slap on the wrist penalties for failure, or assign yourself &quot;tasks&quot; that were nearly impossible to fail with absolutely crushing punishments and reap substantial ongoing rewards in either case.<br /> <br /> Also it was weird that shorter duration avoidance pledges gave you more benefits than long term ones. Other than the (admittedly not trivial) cost of renewing the pledge each day you could reap more benefits while knowing that you had the option to renegotiate each morning if you wanted.<br /> <br /> Back before 2e came out I had a homebrew that used 1e basics with these changes: 1: Duration does not have a numerical effect 2: The value of boons is equal to the <i>greater</i> of tasks or penalties, not the sum","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5328-helmsderp","name":"HelmsDerp","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"652"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-22T00:47:42-07:00","id":1449231,"title":"","content":"Let me state up front I vastly prefer 1e's pledge crafting. It requires a &quot;always watch your words&quot; mindset and rewards cleverness in ways the second edition just doesn't. The way a quick thinking changeling can intentionally warp a conversation and then produce boons or sections out of nowhere fits well with the fairytale inspirations of the game and the fact that your enemies wield the same power only heightens the paranoia. Tomorrow when you show up for work one of your keepers minions could suddenly be your boss, after all.<br /> <br /> The biggest drawback of 1e's pledges is the bookkeeping. As ST you need to write down and remember <i>every</i> pledge your players make as well as those of important NPC's. The need to maintain those tasks is the price of those boons that are so powerful and, in my experience, even the simplest task can become complicated or be accidentally violated in the chaos of game. This becomes doubly important and doubly difficult the second somebody decides to set a trap in a pledge with the intent of cursing the other party.<br /> <br /> One pitfall, mentioned above, is that pledge craft is powerful. Easy access to Status/Allies, in particular, can just blow through many situations which would be challenging obstacles for others. In a recent game, for example, my character was trying to recover an item from the police so they casually promised to get it back &quot;may the Wyrd grant me the authority to recover it&quot; and with their newly minted Police Status they just walked into the station and checked it out in the ordinary fashion.<br /> <br /> The solution, such as it is, is to remember that this is just what changelings do. Vampires can enslave any mortal with the blood bond and changelings can gain access to mortal institutions with a few words. Bear in mind also that they are, primarily, fugitives from the Fae, not from the human world. Three dots of Resources &quot;for free&quot; doesn't really offer much protection against the gentry, it just makes their life nicer while they are hunted.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1448259-playable-fetches?p=1448655#post1448655\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">There's not much point in being a game about being a fugitive trying to rebuild your life while sticking to a consistent locale and working with magic that tries to keep the books balanced if everyone can wordsmith their way to comfort at character creation;</div> </div> </div> </div>This is actually something I\u2019ve always liked about the game. It brings to a head, right from session one, the question of <i>what</i> life you want to build. You can just form a motley and get Resources and a new ID right out the gate but it\u2019s not <i>your</i> life. It\u2019s not the people and things you left behind. Do you just pledge for your old job back or do you kill your fetch and step into their place or do you try to earn it the same way you did the first time?<br /> <br /> As general advice/house rules I do a few things that help with pledge craft.<br /> <br /> 1) The Wyrd can ballance the pledge with Boons and Sactions. This lets the characters only worry about the Task and Duration and you just ballance it behind the scenes if the math dosn\u2019t work out, instead of dropping our of character to calculate everything on the fly.<br /> <br /> 2) Negative Merits. I use them as a thing. More or less a Flaw version of a given Merit but this can come into play when a character uses a Blessing, looses it in some mundane way, and then the pledge runs out. A character that gaind a few dots of Police Status and then lost a dot stealing from evidence might find themselves now a wanted fugative when the loss of the plege-granted dots leaves them with less than zero Status.<br /> <br /> 3) Poison the Boon. I use negative Merits with it and allow for poisoning of boons the partisipants didn\u2019t even receive. This may not be to your taste but I like allowing the players freedom to apply bassically any curse they can think of on a pledge, from \u201cyour fortunes will run dry\u201d (-Resources) to \u201cwe\u2019ll all see how ugly you truly are\u201d (-Striking Looks). I actually just use this entirely in place of the Flaw Sanction.<br /> This may not be a good idea if you\u2019re concerned with how powerful pledges already are - Adding the persision targeting of Merit removal via pledge traps is a whole extra layer of power ontop of everything else you can already do\u2026","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4692-helel","name":"helel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"141"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-22T05:51:46-07:00","id":1449239,"title":"","content":"Thanks for the replies, there are a lot of good points.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>proindrakenzol</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1449175-what-s-wrong-with-1e-pledges-should-i-use-them?p=1449193#post1449193\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">If forging the Pledge is a major focus I would instead use the Social Maneuvering rules. Each party makes an initial offer of terms, this sets the baseline for the Pledge and can modify Doors (or be a complete non-starter). Offering additional services counts as soft leverage, opening all Doors means the other party accepts the Pledge. The PCs can decide whether they accept the Pledge on their own, without the need for social maneuvering (they can also choose to let the NPCs engage in social maneuvering against them).</div> </div> </div> </div>I like that. I always found weird how pledgecraft is described as a skill or as an art in the lore, and yet there is no room for personal ability in the system.<br /> Also, the pledgecrafting scene itslef can become an intersting scene to play.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Satchel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1449175-what-s-wrong-with-1e-pledges-should-i-use-them?p=1449201#post1449201\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">2. Demons occupy a gameplay setup where exposure is more all-or-nothing and pacts are literally one of an extremely small handful of ways to regain Cover; breaking pledges imperils a changeling's Clarity, and making them offers some extra personal security, but pledges aren't going to help you bounce back from penalty-level Clarity, and being able to be out of your mind and still able to hand out Skill bonuses and Striking Looks as an innate template feature is more appropriate for monsters with a literary niche closer to the True Fae than the fugitive person-made-set-dressing trying to gradually rebuild their life in the mortal world.</div> </div> </div> </div>To be fair, one of the selling points of roleplaying any splat is being able to play out the monster's tropes in literature, which for Changeling and Demon includes making deals with hapless mortals. When I played Demon, I couldn't wait to trick someone into selling his soul, because that's what demons do, right? Unfortunately, there is no distinction in literature between lowercase fae and uppercase True Fae. I can see that a player may want to play out the &quot;careful what you wish for&quot; and similar tropes from the fae's point of view, and being able to grant powerful wishes with dreadful sanctions, even though I agree it's more of a True Fae thing.<br /> <br /> I didn't know about the rules in the Summoners book. I could check it out but, as you said, I agree that deals with Supernal/Abyssal/Demonic etc. entities shouldn't be the same type of deals performed by changelings.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>HelmsDerp</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1449175-what-s-wrong-with-1e-pledges-should-i-use-them?p=1449219#post1449219\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Back before 2e came out I had a homebrew that used 1e basics with these changes: 1: Duration does not have a numerical effect 2: The value of boons is equal to the <i>greater</i> of tasks or penalties, not the sum</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bThat's an interesting idea<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>helel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1449175-what-s-wrong-with-1e-pledges-should-i-use-them?p=1449231#post1449231\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The biggest drawback of 1e's pledges is the bookkeeping. As ST you need to write down and remember <i>every</i> pledge your players make as well as those of important NPC's. The need to maintain those tasks is the price of those boons that are so powerful and, in my experience, even the simplest task can become complicated or be accidentally violated in the chaos of game. This becomes doubly important and doubly difficult the second somebody decides to set a trap in a pledge with the intent of cursing the other party.</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't think there is need to use the 1e pledges for evey single pledge made, but only for those that really require them. The ability to warp a casual conversation into a Wyrd-bound pledge is more pertaining to Sealing, and should probably be something quick and easy rules-wise.<br /> Similarly, 2e societal Oaths, like the Freehold, Court and Motley are mostly left in the background, with no need to fill out the details.<br /> <br /> I guess it depends a lot on the type of chronicle you want to play. If it involves a lot of political intrigue then yes, by all means you should watch every word you say and try to keep up with an intricate web of pledges that sooner or later someone is going to break. But if you want to focus your story on something else, less bookkeeping will make the game run smoother.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>helel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1449175-what-s-wrong-with-1e-pledges-should-i-use-them?p=1449231#post1449231\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">1) The Wyrd can ballance the pledge with Boons and Sactions. This lets the characters only worry about the Task and Duration and you just ballance it behind the scenes if the math dosn\u2019t work out, instead of dropping our of character to calculate everything on the fly.</div> </div> </div> </div>That's probably what I would do as well. I don't want to slow down the game to balance everything on the fly, I can do the math off session. Not filling out immediately the details of Sanctions and Boons leaves it to the ST to judge if the pledge cost/benefit is too powerful, leaves a bit of unknown aspect to keep the Wyrd mysterious, unpredictable, and adds a bit of tension for the players.<br /> <br /> <br /> I think what bothers me most about 2e Pledges is 1) Not being able to set a Duration and 2) Not being able to customize your Pledge<br /> It seems weird to me that Duration is no longer a factor involved. For example, you can swear an oath to retrive the Huntsman's heart, without any deadline to do it. Every time the Huntsman comes for you you just keep reassuring &quot;I'm gonna do it, I swear&quot;, and you're not technically breaking your promise.<br /> <br /> As for customization, the types of pledges presented in 2e seem very limited to me. Perhaps you want to come up with something that doesn't fit well with neither Sealing, Oaths or Bargains.<br /> Of course I can make up my own, but I would like to have a simple system general and flexible enough to do that.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":26440,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26440-moonwhisper","user":"moonwhisper","date":". 05-22-2021, 11:01 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/26440-moonwhisper","name":"moonwhisper","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar26440_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2020"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"99"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-22T07:07:50-07:00","id":1449245,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>moonwhisper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1449175-what-s-wrong-with-1e-pledges-should-i-use-them?p=1449239#post1449239\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I guess it depends a lot on the type of chronicle you want to play. If it involves a lot of political intrigue then yes, by all means you should watch every word you say and try to keep up with an intricate web of pledges that sooner or later someone is going to break. But if you want to focus your story on something else, less bookkeeping will make the game run smoother.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's almost the opposite. In political intrigue players (and by assumption NPCs) are very very careful about what they agree to and pledges often wind up written down so everyone can tweak the wording just right. At least that's how my group operates. It's the quick oaths sworn on the fly that are the problem. Somebody swears not to write in the &quot;festive color of blue&quot; and suddenly it matters what color bic the convenience store has on hand for signing the receipt when you use your credit card. For the next year and a day. It's safe and fairly easy to keep and now you, as ST, need to remember it, think about how/when you want to bring it up in play, and adjudicate whether or not blueback counts as blue.<br /> <br /> Or, another example from my past games, I had one player who swore to purchase a small gift for their motley mate every day* in order to get three dots of Resources. Pretty good deal, right? Up until the group got in a fight and she wound up in the ICU for a day and couldn't buy anything. Yes, you could let it slide but a promise is a promise and, from a mechanical perspective, needing to keep that promise is the price the character pays for those Resources. Take that away and changelings really can get near unlimited dots of Merits for absolutely nothing.<br /> <br /> * This actually reminded me of another potential pitfall I house ruled over - Pledges last until their done. This is the Duration, normally, but if you finish all the tasks early the pledge will end early. Cuz you're done. This avoids the &quot;I promise to give you one clover now and get a boon for the rest of my life&quot; exploit.<br /> Hence doing the purchasing every day. And the player really thought her bases were covered because she swore to purchase a gift every day, not give it, so if they were separated she could buy up souvenirs to give when she got home.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4692-helel","name":"helel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"141"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-24T17:57:55-07:00","id":1449497,"title":"","content":"The main downside of 1E Pledgecrafting is they are a minmaxer's dream.<br /> <br /> Played as written it is very easy to take on lots of downsides you won't actually be hindered much by(<i>min</i>imizing the downsides) while taking upsides that are really powerful to you (<i>max</i>imizing the upsides).<br /> <br /> It was supposed to be all about deals and the way the Wyrd balances the scales, but in practice it was just a magic machine to generate goodies from nowhere. A good ST could certainly overrule a lot of that, but, well, a good ST can overrule any broken rule system so that doesn't say much.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2007-wade-l","name":"Wade L","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2007_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"136"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-26T11:23:01-07:00","id":1449676,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>helel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1449175-what-s-wrong-with-1e-pledges-should-i-use-them?p=1449231#post1449231\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Let me state up front I vastly prefer 1e's pledge crafting. It requires a &quot;always watch your words&quot; mindset and rewards cleverness in ways the second edition just doesn't. The way a quick thinking changeling can intentionally warp a conversation and then produce boons or sections out of nowhere fits well with the fairytale inspirations of the game and the fact that your enemies wield the same power only heightens the paranoia. Tomorrow when you show up for work one of your keepers minions could suddenly be your boss, after all.</div> </div> </div> </div>Hard disagree. ^^<br /> &quot;Cleverness&quot; to me is using a limited set of tools in unique and interesting ways. 1E pledges have so much power and inherent possibilities that it's more about picking your solution from a whole bag of easily accessible ones, rather than forcing a solution into being through actual clever manipulation.<br /> <br /> &quot;Always watch your words&quot; is still the norm, the Wyrd is still said to take you at your word at times. My players say a quick &quot;Oh, snap,&quot; when they realized they promised someone something, since they know I (and thus, the Wyrd) am always listening.<br /> <br /> And as for &quot;fairytale inspiration&quot;: nearly no entity in fairytales is so unencumbered as a Changeling with 1E pledges in how they can make deals, and your character's role in the story is not supposed to be one of such an entity either way. I also feel it's just too much on the nose, taking it from &quot;fairytale inspirations&quot; to &quot;fairytale caricature&quot;.<br /> <br /> Find myself nodding along with most of Satchel's points. ^^","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Homebrew</b><br /> - CtL 2E: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1290961-additional-seeming-benefits-for-contracts-2e?p=1454478#post1454478\" target=\"_blank\">Seeming Benefits for every Contract</a>","edited":{"userid":1292,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1292-unahim","user":"Unahim","date":". 05-26-2021, 02:25 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1292-unahim","name":"Unahim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1292_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1080"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-05-27T07:01:22-07:00","id":1449794,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Unahim</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1449175-what-s-wrong-with-1e-pledges-should-i-use-them?p=1449676#post1449676\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">&quot;Cleverness&quot; to me is using a limited set of tools in unique and interesting ways. 1E pledges have so much power and inherent possibilities that it's more about picking your solution from a whole bag of easily accessible ones, rather than forcing a solution into being through actual clever manipulation.</div> </div> </div> </div>I'm curious if you could give an example or two of cleaver manipulation with 2e pledges? You might debate whether or not tricking a cop into breaking a pledge and thereby becoming a wanted criminal during their interrogation of you is &quot;cleaver&quot; with 1e pledges but by my recollection 2e pledges just don't give the tools to even attempt cleverness.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4692-helel","name":"helel","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"141"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-16T12:25:00-07:00","id":1452519,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>helel</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1449175-what-s-wrong-with-1e-pledges-should-i-use-them?p=1449794#post1449794\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I'm curious if you could give an example or two of cleaver manipulation with 2e pledges? You might debate whether or not tricking a cop into breaking a pledge and thereby becoming a wanted criminal during their interrogation of you is &quot;cleaver&quot; with 1e pledges but by my recollection 2e pledges just don't give the tools to even attempt cleverness.</div> </div> </div> </div>I firmly believe that something can only be &quot;clever&quot; if it's perfectly adjusted to the situation. To give an example like you ask would thus be to set up an entire situation sketch to go along with it, and I'm simply not going to do that. ^^<br /> <br /> But to give just a taste: a character in an autocar race, and tricking them into an unwise promise, and then Sealing it with a Willpower to activate Murkblur at a very unfortunate time... does that get the brain juice rolling? ;D<br /> <br /> But note that you are actually misrepresenting my argument. I'm not saying that Pledges are necessarily the path to all cleverness in 2E, but that 1E's Pledges being so powerful <i>prevents</i> any cleverness from occurring with them. They can simply do so much, it's not clever anymore, it's just brute magical power. As I said: clever is the perfect plan for the perfect situation. With 1E Pledges, you can just repeat the same general ideas over and over and over again for almost any situation. Thus... not clever. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/biggrin.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Big Grin\" smilieid=\"3\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> 2E pledges can sometimes set up massive advantages (-3 to the targets Weaponry or Firearm roll for an entire duel is not a small disadvantage... neither is having a Contract activated on them) with clever planning, but they don't fix it all by themselves, requiring even more cleverness to truly capitalize on the opportunity. So... cleverness\u00b2. ;D","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Homebrew</b><br /> - CtL 2E: <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1290961-additional-seeming-benefits-for-contracts-2e?p=1454478#post1454478\" target=\"_blank\">Seeming Benefits for every Contract</a>","edited":{"userid":1292,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1292-unahim","user":"Unahim","date":". 06-16-2021, 03:32 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1292-unahim","name":"Unahim","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1292_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1080"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2021-06-29T21:28:35-07:00","id":1453813,"title":"","content":"Fun fact: If another changeling threatens you, <i>nothing</i> says \u201cI\u2019d like to see you try\u201d quite as well as Sealing it does.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>moonwhisper</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/changeling-the-lost/1449175-what-s-wrong-with-1e-pledges-should-i-use-them?p=1449239#post1449239\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It seems weird to me that Duration is no longer a factor involved. For example, you can swear an oath to retrive the Huntsman's heart, without any deadline to do it. Every time the Huntsman comes for you you just keep reassuring &amp;quot;I'm gonna do it, I swear&amp;quot;, and you're not technically breaking your promise.</div> </div> </div> </div>It\u2019s an incredibly dumb huntsman who doesn\u2019t insist on a deadline or some other assurance and an even dumber changeling who intentionally wastes their one scene of the huntsman having free will (you cannot, in fact, just keep saying \u201cI\u2019ll do it I swear,\u201d or rather you can but only the first time will stop them from throwing that iron fishing net at you).","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13313-sith_happens","name":"Sith_Happens","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13313_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1177"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}